🔍 Key Business Insights
New customers showed the highest churn rate, indicating onboarding and early engagement require improvement.
Customer tenure was identified as one of the strongest indicators of churn.
Customers using computers for login experienced higher churn than mobile users.
Customers from Tier-2 and Tier-3 cities demonstrated higher churn behavior.
Complaint history had a significant impact on customer retention.
Mobile product buyers showed comparatively higher churn.
Customers using Cash on Delivery and E-Wallet payment methods exhibited higher churn risk.
Multiple registered devices were associated with increased churn probability.
Days since the last order emerged as an important retention indicator.
🎯 Customer Segmentation

The project classifies customers into:

New Customers

Recently acquired customers showing high churn probability based on tenure, complaints, payment preferences, login behavior, and purchase activity.

At-Risk Customers

Active customers exhibiting churn characteristics who require proactive retention strategies.

💡 Business Recommendations
Strengthen onboarding experience for new customers.
Improve desktop login experience.
Launch targeted retention campaigns for Tier-2 and Tier-3 cities.
Investigate Cash on Delivery and E-Wallet payment issues.
Improve complaint resolution turnaround time.
Personalize product recommendations based on purchase behavior.
Introduce loyalty programs for high-risk customer segments.
Offer proactive engagement for inactive customers.
Optimize cross-selling and promotional campaigns.
Monitor churn KPIs continuously through dashboards.
